answer:
**Requirements.** `get(key)`, `put(key, value)`, fixed capacity, pluggable eviction (LRU now; LFU, FIFO later), generic key and value types. Clarify thread-safety and TTL expectations.

**Design.**
```java
interface EvictionPolicy<K> {
    void onAccess(K key);     // called on get and put of existing key
    void onInsert(K key);
    void onRemove(K key);
    K evict();                // returns key to remove
}
class Cache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<K, V> store = new HashMap<>();
    private final EvictionPolicy<K> policy;
    V get(K k) { V v = store.get(k); if (v != null) policy.onAccess(k); return v; }
    void put(K k, V v) {
        if (store.containsKey(k)) { store.put(k, v); policy.onAccess(k); return; }
        if (store.size() == capacity) { K victim = policy.evict(); store.remove(victim); }
        store.put(k, v); policy.onInsert(k);
    }
}
```
**LRU policy.** Use a doubly linked list plus a `HashMap<K, Node>`: the head is the most recently used, the tail the least. `onAccess` moves the node to the head in O(1); `evict` removes the tail. In Java `LinkedHashMap` with access order can do this, but interviewers usually want the manual list to see the pointer logic (use sentinel head and tail nodes to avoid null checks).

**SOLID mapping.**
- Single responsibility: `Cache` stores, the policy decides what to evict.
- Open/closed: add `LFUPolicy` without editing `Cache`.
- Liskov and interface segregation: all policies honour the same small interface.
- Dependency inversion: `Cache` depends on the `EvictionPolicy` abstraction, injected through the constructor. A `CacheBuilder` or factory can pick the policy by name.

**Extras.** Make operations thread-safe with a `ReentrantLock` or `synchronized` (a read-write lock does not help LRU because even a `get` mutates order). A separate `StorageBackend` interface allows in-memory vs disk. Optionally add TTL via expiry timestamps checked lazily.

**Test it.** Capacity 2: put A, put B, get A, put C evicts B; updating an existing key does not evict; capacity 1; missing key returns null.
